Don't block tdeinit startup waiting for the launched kded to exit.

Companion to 0005 (force KUniqueApplication no-fork).  tdeinit launches kded and
then calls handle_requests(kded_pid), which waits for that process to DIE — the
fork model's readiness signal (kded forks the daemon and the launched process
exits).  With no-fork forced, the launched kded BECOMES the daemon and never
exits, so this wait blocks forever and hangs all of startup right after KDED is
launched (start_tdeinit_wrapper never returns, ksmserver never starts, the
desktop never comes up).

kded registers with DCOP during its own init and every downstream DCOP call
blocks until it answers, so the explicit death-wait is unnecessary as well as
wrong — skip it so tdeinit proceeds to serve its launcher socket.  With both
patches, starttde reaches the TDE splash, the session manager (ksmserver), and
the kded module set instead of collapsing at the kded launch.

--- a/tdeinit/tdeinit.cpp
+++ b/tdeinit/tdeinit.cpp
@@ -1851,7 +1851,16 @@
 #ifndef NDEBUG
       fprintf(stderr, "[tdeinit] Launched KDED, pid = %ld result = %d\n", (long) pid, d.result);
 #endif
-      handle_requests(pid);
+      /* substrate: KUniqueApplication is forced to the no-fork startup path
+       * (see the tdelibs kuniqueapplication patch — the fork path's forked
+       * daemon loses its DCOP connection on substrate).  In no-fork mode the
+       * launched kded BECOMES the daemon and never exits, but handle_requests()
+       * here waits for that very process to DIE (the fork model's "child forked,
+       * parent exited" readiness signal).  That wait now blocks forever and
+       * hangs all of startup right after KDED launches.  kded registers with
+       * DCOP during its own init and every downstream DCOP call blocks until it
+       * answers, so this explicit death-wait is both wrong and unnecessary —
+       * skip it and let tdeinit proceed to serve its launcher socket. */
    }
 
    for(i = 1; i < argc; i++)
